023529052734375, 0.02655029296875, -0.01103973388671875, -0.01273345947265625, 0.0038356781005859375, -0.020172119140625, 0.00850677490234375, -0.0050048828125, 0.00524139404296875, 0.0037784576416015625, 0.01067352294921875, -0.0162811279296875, 0.00885009765625, 0.026641845703125, 0.015594482421875, -0.0003879070281982422, -0.0164337158203125, 0.00909423828125, -0.00998687744140625, 0.0162353515625, -0.017730712890625, 0.00295257568359375, -0.0059661865234375, -0.023468017578125, 0.0088348388671875, 0.004184722900390625, -0.002838134765625, -0.0093536376953125, 0.03955078125, 0.01432037353515625, 0.01088714599609375, -0.0184173583984375, 0.005077362060546875, 0.01438140869140625, -0.005695343017578125, -0.005802154541015625, 0.00830078125, -0.02899169921875, 0.005046844482421875, -0.01146697998046875, -0.0016794204711914062, -0.01061248779296875, -0.019012451171875, 0.0224151611328125, -0.044403076171875, 0.00196075439453125, -0.0271148681640625, -0.0259246826171875, 0.00814056396484375, -0.03326416015625, -0.0024929046630859375, -0.01546478271484375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked. In this work, we address the following research questions: (1) How robust are memory-based self-improving agents to stochasticity and task order? (2) To what extent does task and environment specification mitigate observed fragility? (3) What evaluation protocols and oversight mechanisms can improve the reliability of these agents?\n\n**Background**  \nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: (a) Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA), which store and retrieve textual summaries of past task experiences, and (b) Self-Reflective Memory Agents (SRMA), which maintain a memory bank of self-generated improvement notes. These methods have been widely adopted in recent work for continual learning and self-improvement in complex environments.\n\n**Methods**  \nTo comprehensively re-evaluate these methods, we broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple runs to quantify variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the tasks to investigate the effect of task order. Specifically, we conduct experiments in two simulated environments\u2014GridWorld and TextWorld\u2014using a suite of 20 multi-step tasks per environment. For each method, we perform 10 independent runs per task ordering, and for each run, we randomly shuffle the task sequence to assess sensitivity to curriculum. Agent performance is measured by average task completion rate and cumulative reward.\n\nTo analyze the agents' memory, we manually examine the memory bank contents after each run. This involves coding memory entries for specificity, relevance, and correctness using a rubric developed for this study. Two annotators independently review the memory logs and resolve disagreements through discussion.\n\n**Results**  \nOur experiments yield two key observations that expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks: for example, the standard deviation of task completion rates across runs is as high as 12% for EMAA and 15% for SRMA in TextWorld. Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, with some runs showing performance drops of up to 20% relative to the mean. Second, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order. When tasks are shuffled, average completion rates drop by 10\u201318% compared to the default curriculum order, indicating that prior works' default orderings impose an implicit curriculum that acts as a hidden prerequisite for success.\n\nManual examination of the agents' memory reveals that underspecified tasks and environments lead to vague or misleading memory entries, which in turn propagate errors in subsequent tasks. For example, in 40% of failed runs, memory entries lacked actionable details or contained incorrect assumptions about the environment. To validate the hypothesis that underspecification contributes to fragility, we incorporate additional information\u2014such as detailed rubrics and explicit environment feedback\u2014into the memory construction process. This intervention improves average completion rates by 6\u20139%, partially closing the performance degradation observed in previous experiments. However, significant gaps still remain: even with enhanced specification, completion rates under random task orderings are still 8\u201312% lower than under the default curriculum, and variance across runs remains high.\n\n**Discussion**  \nThese findings suggest that other uncharacterized factors, such as memory interference, suboptimal retrieval strategies, or limitations in the agents' reasoning capabilities, may also contribute to fragility. Future work should investigate the impact of memory management algorithms, retrieval heuristics, and the integration of external knowledge sources.\n\nTo improve reliability, we recommend the following concrete steps: (1) Always report results across multiple independent runs and task orderings to capture variance and curriculum effects; (2) Develop standardized benchmarks with randomized task sequences; (3) Incorporate structured rubrics and environment feedback into agent memory; (4) Design interfaces that allow human reviewers to audit and edit agent memory entries, enabling effective oversight and early detection of failure modes.\n\nIn summary, our work advocates for more rigorous and transparent evaluation protocols for self-improving agents and highlights the need for systems and interfaces that support effective human oversight, thereby preventing agents from failing in unforeseeable ways."}}